/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111825 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $y=2^x.$ First, we create a table of values:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y = 2^x$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e987a8647092c836bde4bcea31dc075e80e9da36a67133fc2b9431b99c5e42ed.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1\\. The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1).$\n\n2\\. The graph is increasing.\n\n3\\. For $x>0,$ the graph grows very rapidly as $x$ increases. Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow \\infty.$\n\n4\\. For $x<0,$ the graph gets very close to zero as $x$ decreases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has the horizontal asymptote $y=0.$ Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\n5\\. The domain is $x\\in (-\\infty , \\infty )$, and the range is $y>0.$\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve $y=a^x$ with $a>1.$ These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/48/48f7bc7bb25bf1955d1e2cf48cffbf09e17f80a1228cada1c31f7ad4822bdace.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111825 #edb/ref 1065 1540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111825 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1a4d886d-d7b8-5667-b52a-2c2eb4a35b91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111825 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111826 #edb/ref 1064 "Multiplying an exponential growth function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve ![](images/68/68dcb337cb0927d43403eebeb21c0a54dc46a136f7cd21ce821d9bfc325c748e.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) by a factor of ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)  It takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg) This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/70/7052037576cfaf29807f0ad332100b9929a72806846219da2599c8f911500d87.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remained the same, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moved from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/43/437619dfcf8db5be350de504dc9d98eab13c2ac21c62998d3c268a1f475a30d9.svg)\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve ![](images/8c/8ccfcf186ac190ccb8f91676958e6030b4224bfd46b3598261736fbfcb7c351f.svg)\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remains the same, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moves from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111826 #edb/ref 1065 1541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111826 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8c1fa510-e5f3-585b-852a-c46724144ec1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111826 #edb/ref 1067 "Vertical Stretching" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111833 #edb/ref 1064 "The curve ![](images/e9/e910a01694746b83e1662e73779d7e758399e2d0bdf7441bbd8221a37f278b17.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) by a factor of ![](images/79/79cadb0dadf367d4c7456e91f5847fa57d3f90d5129f383ca6c073782ecc6892.svg) It takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/79/79cadb0dadf367d4c7456e91f5847fa57d3f90d5129f383ca6c073782ecc6892.svg) This has the effect of squashing, or **compressing**, the curve downwards.\n\n![](images/68/685e360ed7bcd7975aa705d192ef487af17688bb113b20e10f881a0bdf7f46b7.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remained the same, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moved from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/97/97a51f546ed69cb656e8a811f3a4acf3f6dacce8e9ed7cac63419ad63f0497d1.svg)\n\nIn general for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve ![](images/8c/8ccfcf186ac190ccb8f91676958e6030b4224bfd46b3598261736fbfcb7c351f.svg)\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remains the same, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moves from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111833 #edb/ref 1065 1858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111833 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "78c17e05-8ef3-58fd-a9ea-a2fc04f4bd83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111833 #edb/ref 1067 "Vertical Compression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570115 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186570818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570115 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186570820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570115 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186570867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570115 #edb/ref 